{"name":"Nicotine Glaze","hex":"#cc7755","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nicotine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An alkaloid (C10H14N2), commonly occurring in the tobacco plant.","Tobacco or cigarettes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nicotine"},{"term":"glaze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The vitreous coating of pottery or porcelain; anything used as a coating or color in glazing.","A transparent or semi-transparent layer of paint."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To install windows.","To apply a thin, transparent layer of coating."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glaze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Nicotine","blocks":[{"text":"Nicotine is a stimulant and alkaloid found primarily in plants of the nightshade family, notably in tobacco; it is also synthesized. Nicotine is used recreationally for its stimulant and anxiolytic effects. In tobacco leaves, nicotine constitutes about 0.6–3% of the dry weight, and smaller, trace quantities occur in other Solanaceae crops such as tomatoes, potatoes, and eggplants. In pure form, nicotine is a colorless to yellowish, oily liquid that readily penetrates biological membranes and acts as a potent neurotoxin in insects, where it serves as an antiherbivore toxin. Historically, it was widely used as an insecticide, and its structure provided the basis for synthetic neonicotinoid pesticides."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Nicotine_structure.svg/330px-Nicotine_structure.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicotine","wikidata":"Q28086552"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Nicotine_molecule_ball_from_xtal.png/500px-Nicotine_molecule_ball_from_xtal.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Ni